<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <RootNamespace>GlobalConversationTracker.Session.Tests</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>GlobalConversationTracker.Session.Tests</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.11.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="2.8.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference
      Include="..\GlobalConversationTracker.Session\GlobalConversationTracker.Session.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    A BUILD TRIGGER, NOT TEST COVERAGE. Nothing here tests the plugin, and
    nothing here can: it needs BepInEx, the IL2CPP interop assemblies and a
    running game. This reference exists for one reason, stated plainly so that
    nobody reads it as a claim about what is tested.

    `dotnet test` builds the test projects and what they reference, and nothing
    referenced the plugin - so a compile error in it passed the standard
    end-of-change check while `dotnet build` caught it. Two commands where one
    would do is a rule people forget; a project reference is a rule the build
    remembers for them.

    ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" is what keeps it honest: MSBuild keeps the
    build-order dependency and drops everything else, so these tests are not
    compiled against the plugin, cannot use a type from it, and do not get its
    DLL copied into their output. The only observable effect is that the plugin
    must compile before these tests run.

    This adds no new requirement on a checkout: Session and Persistence already
    reference assemblies out of a game install, and this project already
    references Session, so `dotnet test` has needed a resolvable install since
    long before this line (see Directory.Build.props for how one is found).

    Chosen here rather than in another test project because Session is the last
    layer under the plugin; if that ever stops being true, move the line rather
    than adding a second one.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference
      Include="..\GlobalConversationTracker.Plugin\GlobalConversationTracker.Plugin.csproj"
      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
